2026 BAU Engineering Contributions – Bill Pay, Wires & Deposit Money Movement
1. Architecture and Reusable Patterns
1.1 WireMock Sidecar Container (adopted across 7 APIs)

One-liner: Designed a reusable WireMock sidecar-container architecture that took test dependencies out of production artifacts, cleared the related Nexus IQ findings, and was adopted across all seven team-owned APIs.

Context:

Our Bill Pay and Wires applications use WireMock for integration and acceptance testing. Its transitive dependencies were being flagged by Nexus IQ for security vulnerabilities, because test-only libraries had become part of the application’s packaging footprint.
Fixing each finding through upgrades or exclusions wasn’t sustainable. It meant repeating the same fixes across seven APIs, with a risk of inconsistent implementations. Future WireMock releases would also bring in new transitive dependencies, so the problem would keep coming back.
I separated the mock server from the application runtime. WireMock now runs as its own container in the same OpenShift pod, the application reaches it over localhost, and Helm/OpenShift configuration manages the setup.
This fixed the coupling itself, not just the current list of vulnerabilities. Integration and acceptance testing were fully preserved. The WireMock image is still maintained and scanned on its own, but it’s no longer bundled with the production application.
Outcome: WireMock-related Nexus IQ findings were removed from application artifacts, there’s one standard pattern across seven APIs, and recurring remediation effort went down.
1.2 Composite Trust Manager – FIS Certificate Transition (4 Bill Pay APIs)

One-liner: Diagnosed the FIS certificate-trust issue in one day and delivered a Composite Trust Manager across four Bill Pay APIs in three days, ahead of FIS’s Sectigo certificate transition, without weakening TLS security.

Context:

In May–June 2026, FIS moved its TLS certificate chain to new Sectigo root and intermediate certificates. Our Bill Pay APIs call FIS through Apache CXF SOAP clients via the corporate proxy.
Working with the proxy team, I confirmed the proxy runs in pass-through mode. That meant the proxy couldn’t handle certificate trust, so validating FIS’s certificate chain was the application’s job. Without a fix, FIS calls would fail with SSLHandshakeException / PKIX path building failed.
Why not replace the truststore: a custom FIS-only truststore could break other outbound HTTPS connections that rely on Java’s standard trusted CAs.
Why not disable verification: not acceptable, because it would compromise TLS server authentication. The existing KeyManager setup only handles our client certificate and doesn’t validate FIS’s server certificate.
I designed a Composite Trust Manager that keeps both trust sources. Default Java trust continues for standard CAs, and a custom FIS trust adds the required Sectigo chain. A certificate is accepted only when one of the configured trust paths validates it. The existing client-certificate setup was left unchanged.
Outcome: one shared solution instead of separate fixes per API. I coordinated UAT and production rollout across four Bill Pay APIs within three days, ahead of the June 7, 2026 FIS certificate activation, keeping FIS connectivity secure. It also gives us a reusable approach for future certificate changes.
