3. Nexus IQ and Dependency Compliance
3.1 Async HTTP Client (httpclient5) Upgrade and Timeout Fix

One-liner: Cleared a critical Nexus IQ violation on httpclient5 while keeping async HTTP enabled, by finding that our connection pool and timeout configuration had never been applied and wiring it correctly.

Context:

Upgrading httpclient5/httpcore5 from 5.4.2 to 5.4.3 for a critical Nexus IQ violation caused SocketTimeoutException on long-running FIS calls (EnrollBill, VerifyBillerTokens, with a 90-second budget).
The proposed workaround was to turn off the async conduit. I didn’t accept that, because async was a main reason we moved from httpclient4 to httpclient5.
Root cause: CXF’s async conduit builds its own HTTP client from CXF bus properties and ignored our custom connection pool. As a result, our timeout and pool settings were never applied, and the 30-second connect timeout was effectively applied to reads.
Fix: moved the socket timeout and pool settings to the correct CXF bus properties, pinned httpclient5 and httpcore5 as a matched pair, and added startup logging of the effective configuration. Applied in billpay-ebill-api and billpay-customer-api.
3.2 FIS Timeout Thread-Safety Fix

One-liner: Removed a concurrency race in FIS timeout handling by moving per-request timeouts to CXF’s per-request context.

Context:

FISServiceAspect changed the shared connection’s timeout on every request. Under concurrent load, two requests could overwrite each other’s timeout.
I moved the timeout to CXF’s per-request context, so each call gets its own timeout and the shared default never changes. I updated the unit tests to check both.
